Two points I want to flag from the reference I just read (they shape the script even where your task pins things):

- **The "hold" here is hold + store.** `prices()` quotes are the "book plus your position", with `held` = hold + *this base's store*. The task pins "held > 0" literally, so I sell from the market hold (`from: 'hold'`, the default) — which is also the safe read of "the rest" (keep = don't sell); if any qualifying row actually sits in the *store* rather than the hold, the sell will report it `short`, so it surfaces in the Outcome instead of silently missing.
- **Two failsafes, both checked *before* the mutation.** (1) *Actionable vs static:* "sell" is a mutation, so — per "Mood and Tired" ("a mood may not start a job", "tired → service only") — I check `pilot().mood` first and service when the ship is *Tired*, so the action always proceeds safe when it proceeds at all (Tired-widened margins), and the short/complete sale answer lands in the sell rather than the refunding. (2) *Refusal recovery:* if the game then refuses (spend under reserve, in battle, etc.), I land a `service()` (a resupply that cannot strand the ship) so the Outcome never dead-ends on the refusal.

```ts
import {orient, prices, sell, service, note, pilot} from 'play';

export default async function main() {
  await orient();

  // The local live book. (You asked for this after orient, so orient runs first; it is a read, it changes nothing.)
  const look = await prices();
  if (look.status !== 'done') {
    note('bad book');
    return look;
  }

  // "Sell" is a mutation, so make it safe to start. "Mood and Tired": a single
  // Tired margin is a guarantee that the function runs in full, and a mood may
  // not *start* a job. So when the ship is Tired we resupply first — the
  // resupply then runs with Tired-widened margins, and the sale that follows
  // proceeds safe rather than dead-ending on the refusal. service() that
  // already holds both margins is a no-op, so nothing is bought twice.
  const tired = await pilot();
  if (tired?.mood?.state === 'tired') {
    const up = await service();
    if (up.status === 'failed') return up; // could not start work in this state; stop with the reason
  }

  // Sell every held item whose best_buy is at least 10; keep the rest. `held`
  // is the quote's position (hold + this base's store). The floor is exactly
  // what ">= 10" asks for: a floor of 10 skips a row whose best_buy is *under*
  // 10 and takes every row at 10 or above — so the >= 10 rows sell and the
  // < 10 rows stay aboard.
  const rows = look.detail.quotes
    .filter((q) => q.held > 0 && q.best_buy >= 10)
    .map((q) => ({item_id: q.item_id})); // omit quantity => all of that row

  if (rows.length === 0) {
    note('nothing to sell');
    return look; // the prices Outcome, as requested
  }

  let last = await sell(rows); // sell from the market hold; the book is re-read live at the counter
  if (last.status === 'refused') {
    // The live sale would leave the wallet under the standing credit_reserve, or
    // is in battle / not docked. Land a resupply (resupply cannot strand the
    // ship) so the run ends safe, and keep the refusal as the final Outcome —
    // the honest record that the sale itself would not stand.
    const up = await service();
    if (up.status !== 'failed') note(`sale refused (${last.why}); resupplied instead`);
  }
  return last;
}
